  "factVenus2": "Venus is hotter than Mercury, even though it is farther from the Sun, because its thick air traps the heat.",
  "factEarth1": "Earth is the only place we know with life, and about 71% of its surface is covered by water.",
  "factEarth2": "Earth is not a perfect ball. It bulges a little at the equator because it spins.",
  "factMars1": "Olympus Mons on Mars is the tallest volcano in the solar system, about two and a half times as tall as Mount Everest.",
  "factMars2": "Sunsets on Mars look blue.",
  "factJupiter1": "Jupiter has more than twice the mass of all the other planets put together.",
  "factJupiter2": "The Great Red Spot is a storm bigger than Earth, and people have watched it for more than 150 years.",
  "factSaturn1": "Saturn is so light for its size that it would float in water, if you could find a bathtub big enough.",
  "factSaturn2": "Saturn's rings are very wide but very thin: in places only about ten metres thick.",
  "factUranus1": "Uranus rolls around the Sun on its side, tilted by about 98 degrees.",
  "factUranus2": "Uranus was the first planet found with a telescope. William Herschel spotted it in 1781.",
  "factNeptune1": "Neptune has the fastest winds of any planet, more than 2,000 km/h.",
  "factNeptune2": "Neptune was found with maths before anyone saw it: in 1846 astronomers worked out where it had to be.",
  "factMoon1": "The Moon moves about 3.8 centimetres farther from Earth every year.",
  "factMoon2": "We always see the same side of the Moon, because it turns exactly once for each trip around Earth.",
  "factPluto1": "A year on Pluto lasts about 248 Earth years.",
  "factPluto2": "Pluto has a huge heart-shaped plain of frozen nitrogen.",
  "factIo1": "Io is the most volcanic world we know, with hundreds of active volcanoes.",
  "factIo2": "Jupiter's gravity squeezes and stretches Io, and the heat from this powers its volcanoes.",
  "factEuropa1": "Under its icy shell, Europa probably holds about twice as much water as all of Earth's oceans.",
  "factEuropa2": "Europa is one of the best places to look for life beyond Earth. The Europa Clipper spacecraft is on its way there.",
  "factGanymede1": "Ganymede is the largest moon in the solar system. It is even bigger than Mercury.",
  "factGanymede2": "Ganymede is the only moon known to have a magnetic field of its own.",
  "factCallisto1": "Callisto is one of the most heavily cratered places in the solar system, and its surface is very old.",
  "factCallisto2": "Callisto is far enough from Jupiter to get less of its harsh radiation, so scientists have suggested it for a future base.",
  "factTitan1": "Titan is the only moon with a thick atmosphere. Its air pressure at the surface is higher than on Earth.",
  "factTitan2": "Titan has rivers and lakes, but they hold liquid methane and ethane, not water.",
  "factEnceladus1": "Enceladus shoots jets of ice and water vapour into space through cracks near its south pole.",
  "factEnceladus2": "The jets of Enceladus feed Saturn's E ring.",
  "factTriton1": "Triton goes around Neptune backwards, so it was probably a dwarf planet that Neptune captured.",
  "factTriton2": "At about −235 °C, Triton is one of the coldest places anyone has measured in the solar system.",
